sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch04-21> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Sensitive dependence on initial conditions (the chaos phenomenon) only amplifies differences already present in initial conditions, so on its own it contributes nothing to the ultimate production of randomness, even in systems that also show intrinsic randomness.";
    dc:type "general-principle";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Sensitive dependence on initial conditions does not itself produce randomness";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 4, “Systems Based on Numbers”; section “Continuous Cellular Automata” (from p. 155), p. 155. Scope: chaotic dynamical systems and iterated maps"
}
